{
  "result": "There's a particular stillness that arrives just after sunset, when the sky has spent its last bright colors and the streetlamps begin their slow, faithful work. It is not yet night, no longer day\u2014an in-between hour that makes the world feel porous, as if a secret might slip through if you listen carefully enough.\n\nI like these moments because they ask nothing of me. The morning is for ambition, for lists and deadlines and the sharp edges of purpose. But dusk is honest. It admits that not everything needs to be finished. The dishes can wait in the sink. The unanswered email can remain unanswered for one more hour. Outside, someone walks a dog past my window; their footsteps are soft and unhurried, like punctuation at the end of a long sentence.\n\nI think we are taught to fear stillness, as though it is a kind of failure. We fill it with podcasts and scrolling, with noise that pretends to be company. But the quiet has its own grammar. It lets you notice the way light bends across the ceiling, the distant hum of a refrigerator, the small weight of your own breathing. These are not distractions. They are the texture of being alive.\n\nSometimes I write in this hour, not because I have something important to say, but because the page listens better than the day does. Words come slower, truer. They do not need to perform. And when I finally look up, the room has grown dark around me, and I feel, for a moment, perfectly located in time.",
